<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/intermediate/the-heart-and-lungs-in-exercise">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The last lesson looked inside the muscle factory; this one is its
    <strong>supply chain</strong>. The cardiorespiratory system — heart, blood vessels,
    lungs — delivers oxygen and fuel to working muscle and hauls the waste away, and
    its adaptation profile (stroke volume up, capillaries up, threshold up) is the
    measurable story of getting fitter. The supply chain framing explains why
    endurance sports look the way they do: when the limiting factor is delivery
    capacity, training becomes an engineering project to widen every pipe in the
    system — bigger pump, more blood, denser capillary networks, more mitochondria
    to spend what arrives. This page covers the respiratory system in exercise, the
    circulatory system and the cardiac-output equation, VO₂max as the delivery
    ceiling, the acute and chronic effects of exercise on the body, the lactate
    threshold as the performance number, heart-rate zones used honestly, and the
    altitude protocols that try to buy red blood cells legally.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the equation, the adaptations, the threshold, altitude" widget="equationStep" config={cardiopulCfg} />

  <h2>The respiratory system: big margins at the top</h2>
  <p>
    Breathing moves air past the alveoli — the thin-walled sacs where oxygen and
    carbon dioxide diffuse between air and blood — and ventilation is measured as
    the product of <strong>tidal volume</strong> (air per breath) and <strong>rate</strong>. At rest,
    that is roughly 0.5 L × 12 breaths/min ≈ 6 L/min. In maximal exercise, healthy
    adults reach 100 L/min and more; elite endurance athletes exceed
    <strong>150–200 L/min</strong>. The numbers tell the strategic story: the respiratory
    system has enormous headroom, and in most healthy people it is
    <strong>not the limiting factor</strong> in maximal performance — arterial blood
    normally leaves the lungs about 95–98% saturated with oxygen even at
    maximal effort (elite athletes in extreme cases show small desaturation,
    the "exercise-induced arterial hypoxaemia" that hints even their pumps are
    outrunning their lungs). Two respiratory realities do matter to athletes.
    First, the <strong>respiratory muscles fatigue</strong>: sustaining ventilation near
    150 L/min costs work, and heavily working breathing muscles steal blood flow
    from the limbs (the body pays for breathing from the same cardiac output) —
    one reason inspiratory-muscle training shows a small real benefit. Second,
    the <strong>breathing response is chemically governed</strong>: rising CO₂ (more than
    falling O₂) drives the urge to breathe, which is why breath-holding in
    swimming is dangerous in a way dry-land breath games are not — the warning
    signal can be blunted by pre-hyperventilating, and blackout follows with
    little notice.
  </p>

  <h2>The circulatory system: the delivery equation</h2>
  <p>
    The heart moves the oxygen cargo the lungs load. Its output obeys the
    fundamental equation: <strong>cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume</strong>
    (Q = HR × SV). At rest: about 70 bpm × 70 mL ≈ 5 L/min. In maximal exercise
    in an untrained adult: about 200 bpm × 100–110 mL ≈ 20–22 L/min. In an elite
    endurance athlete: the same maximum heart rate (perhaps even slightly lower)
    with a stroke volume near <strong>200 mL</strong> — a cardiac output of
    <strong>35–40 L/min</strong>, roughly double. That difference is the single largest
    physiological gap between the fit and the elite, and it is built two ways:
    the <strong>left ventricle enlarges</strong> with years of volume loading (the
    "athlete's heart" — bigger chamber, thicker walls proportionate to the
    work), and <strong>blood volume expands</strong> — plasma volume rises within weeks
    of endurance training, which fills the bigger pump and, as a bonus, aids
    temperature regulation. The circulation also <strong>redistributes</strong>: at
    maximal exercise, working muscle receives 80–85% of cardiac output (versus
    about 15–20% at rest) while gut and kidneys accept less — a regulated
    shunting via vasoconstriction and dilation. And the cargo itself:
    haemoglobin at about 150 g/L of blood carries roughly 200 mL of oxygen per
    litre, so a 5 L blood volume holds about a litre of oxygen in transit at
    any instant — a quantity the trained system turns over many times each
    minute.
  </p>

  <h2>VO₂max: the delivery ceiling</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>VO₂max</strong> — the maximum rate of oxygen consumption, expressed per
    kilogram per minute — is the delivery system's dyno test: how much oxygen
    the whole chain (lungs, heart, blood, muscle mitochondria) can consume at
    maximal effort. Untrained young adults sit around 35–45 mL/kg/min; regular
    recreational runners 50–60; elite male distance runners and cross-country
    skiers <strong>70–90+</strong>, with the highest credibly measured values near
    <strong>96–97</strong> (Norwegian cyclist Oskar Svendsen's 2012 lab test is the
    commonly cited record). The classic finding — established by decades of
    work including the cross-circulation experiments that traded one animal's
    blood flow to another — is that <strong>maximal cardiac output is the anchor
    of the ceiling</strong>: infuse extra blood volume and VO₂max rises; remove
    some and it falls; altitude acclimatisation's benefits ride on the same
    lever. But the ceiling is not destiny, for two reasons. Peripheral
    adaptations (mitochondria, capillaries) determine how much of the delivered
    oxygen the muscle can actually use. And distance performance depends more
    on the <strong>fraction of the ceiling you can sustain</strong> — the threshold —
    than on the ceiling itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The lactate threshold: the number that predicts races</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>lactate threshold</strong> (physiologists distinguish lactate
    threshold, the first sustained rise, from the higher "maximal lactate
    steady state"; coaches compress them into "threshold pace") marks the
    intensity above which blood lactate climbs persistently — the point where
    the aerobic system can no longer balance production with clearance. Below
    it, you can run for hours; above it, fatigue compounds in minutes. The
    untrained reach threshold around <strong>55–65% of VO₂max</strong>; trained
    distance athletes sustain <strong>80–90%</strong> — and elite marathoners race
    42.2 km at close to their threshold. The training that raises it is the
    now-familiar stack: easy-volume work builds the aerobic base and
    mitochondria; tempo runs at threshold intensity teach the system to
    clear lactate at higher rates; interval work pushes the ceiling itself.
    Physiologists can measure the threshold precisely (blood samples against
    treadmill speed), but the field versions are honest enough: the pace you
    could hold for about an hour (the classic 20-minute test), or the point
    where conversation becomes impossible.
  </p>

  <h2>Acute responses and chronic adaptations: exercise's effects on the body</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's "effects of exercise on the body" splits into the
    <strong>acute response</strong> (what happens in one session) and
    <strong>chronic adaptation</strong> (what training builds). Acutely: heart rate
    rises before the muscles even start (anticipatory feed-forward from the
    nervous system), then climbs in proportion to intensity until it plateaus
    at maximum; stroke volume climbs with it until SV maxes around 40–50% of
    VO₂max, leaving heart rate to carry the rest; ventilation rises with a
    fast neural phase then a slower chemical drift; systolic blood pressure
    rises substantially (to 200+ mmHg in maximal lifts) while diastolic stays
    near constant in dynamic exercise; blood is shunted to skin for cooling
    and to muscle for work; and plasma volume shrinks slightly as water
    leaves the blood for sweating muscle — the dehydration drift the
    nutrition lesson manages. Chronically, weeks to months of training
    deliver: plasma volume up (the fastest adaptation, within days to
    weeks), mitochondria and capillaries up in muscle (the engine and the
    pipes), stroke volume and cardiac output up, resting heart rate down
    (from the bigger pump idling — 40–50 bpm in serious endurance athletes
    is unremarkable), blood pressure modestly down, and insulin sensitivity
    up. Resting measures are also the honest cheap tests: a rising resting
    heart rate across days signals fatigue, illness or under-fuelling.
  </p>

  <h2>Heart-rate zones, used honestly</h2>
  <p>
    Zone training works as a dial — the five-zone model (recovery, easy,
    tempo, threshold, VO₂max work) organises intensity, and polarised
    training distributions (roughly 80% easy, 20% hard) are well supported
    in elite practice. But the arithmetic underneath carries a known flaw:
    the popular <strong>220 − age</strong> maximum-heart-rate formula has an
    individual error of about <strong>±10–12 bpm</strong> — it predicts the
    average, not you (better-fitting formulas such as Tanaka's
    208 − 0.7 × age reduce but do not remove the spread), and a 10-bpm
    error shifts every zone. The professional fix is a <strong>field test</strong>:
    measure your own maximum (a hard, progressive test, done safely) or,
    better, anchor zones to threshold pace or power and use heart rate as
    a drift gauge (cardiac drift — the slow upward creep at constant pace
    in heat and dehydration — tells you about the day, not the zones).
  </p>

  <h2>Altitude: buying red blood cells legally</h2>
  <p>
    At altitude, thinner air means less oxygen per breath, and the body's
    answer is <strong>erythropoietin (EPO)</strong>: the kidney signals, the bone
    marrow builds red blood cells, and haemoglobin mass climbs — the same
    lever the banned drug rHuEPO pulls artificially, which is why altitude
    is legal and the injection is not (the anti-doping line is a rule
    about administration, not chemistry). The evidence-based protocol,
    established by Levine and Stray-Gundersen's controlled 1997 study,
    is <strong>live high, train low</strong>: live or sleep at roughly
    2,000–2,500 m (enough hypoxia to trigger erythropoiesis) for about
    four weeks, but descend to train below about 1,250 m so interval
    quality survives. The payoff at sea level is roughly
    <strong>1–3%</strong> — small, but decisive in championship fields.
    The compromises are real: altitude tents and hotel rooms simulate
    the live-high half at home; responders vary substantially (some
    athletes' haemoglobin barely moves); and the dose must be managed,
    since excessive erythropoiesis thickens blood and raises clot risk —
    one reason governing bodies cap permitted haemoglobin levels in
    competition.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring the ceiling: the lab and the field</h2>
  <p>
    The physiology above only matters if it can be measured, and the
    measurement has its own craft. The <strong>laboratory VO₂max test</strong>
    is a progressive ramp to exhaustion (treadmill or cycle) with
    expired-air analysis: oxygen inhaled minus oxygen exhaled, plotted
    against workload, with VO₂max declared when oxygen uptake plateaus
    despite rising speed — the gold standard, and unpleasant by design
    (elite tests are terminated by the athlete's collapse more often
    than by the protocol). Field versions trade a little accuracy for
    a lot of convenience: the <strong>Copper 12-minute run</strong> and
    the <strong>beep test</strong> (20 m shuttles, levels and laps) estimate
    VO₂max from work done; the <strong>step test</strong> estimates it from
    heart-rate recovery. All are good enough to track a season —
    the trend is the athlete's information; the absolute number is
    the lab's. The practical reading order for any performer:
    establish the ceiling once, then train the sustainable fraction
    (the threshold section above) and re-test the field marker
    every six to eight weeks — the number that predicts races
    is the one that moves.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The athlete's heart versus the disease.</strong> Years of training
    remodel the heart — chamber enlargement, wall thickening, benign
    arrhythmias — and sports cardiology's daily task is distinguishing
    adaptation from cardiomyopathy and other dangerous conditions. The
    stakes are concrete: sudden cardiac death in young athletes is rare
    (roughly 1 in 50,000–100,000 athlete-years) but usually caused by
    undiagnosed structural disease such as hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.
    Screening policy splits on it: Italy mandates ECG screening for
    competitive athletes and reports falling death rates; US practice
    relies on history and examination, with ECG screening debated on
    cost and false-positive grounds.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The delivery-limit debate.</strong> How much of VO₂max is central
    (cardiac output) versus peripheral (mitochondria) remains a live
    argument, sharpened by experiments that manipulate one limb while
    measuring the other; the working synthesis is that the central limit
    dominates in whole-body exercise, with peripheral capacity deciding
    local muscle endurance and the sustainable fraction. Meanwhile the
    questions moving fastest are practical: heat acclimation as a legal
    plasma-volume booster, carbon-monoxide rebreathing to measure
    haemoglobin mass (and its abuse potential), and individual
    responder prediction for altitude camps.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Levine on the athlete's heart; Bassett &amp; Whyte on the VO₂max history; the medicine ladder's circulation lesson; the altitude literature (Levine &amp; Stray-Gundersen, 1997).</p>

